GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\glm_mini_p2.md:3:FACTS.  m88k_exception(cpu, vector, is_trap) has a first switch on `vector` where the fix adds, for seven classes (MISALIGNED_ACCESS=4, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE=5, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION=6, BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION=7, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE=8, INTEGER_OVERFLOW=9, ERROR=10), the statement `implemented = true;`.  A fallback after the switch stops every guest CPU when `vector < 128 && !implemented`.  A second (tail) switch gained bare `case` labels for the same seven so they no longer hit its `default:` (which stops this CPU).  Two routes reach the first switch: (A) INSTRUCTION faults (is_trap == 0: divu by zero, add overflow, misaligned ld, user-mode prom_call, user-mode ldcr) and (B) a supervisor `tb0`/`tb1`/`tcnd` TRAP whose 9-bit field names the vector directly (is_trap == 1) -- the MC88100 manual 6.5.5 says a supervisor trap may name any vector.  The 10-row detector's rows: div0/ovf/misalign (route A, supervisor), priv/privtb/unimpl (route A, user mode), bounds (route B: tb1 naming 7 -- vector 7 has NO instruction raise site), error (route: a fault inside a handler with SFRZ set re-routes to vector 10 AFTER the first switch), errortb (route B naming 10), dslot (route A in a delay slot).  Each row asserts the machine is still running, pc == 8*vector, SXIP = faulting pc | 2, SNIP = pc+4 | 2, SFIP = SNIP+4, PSR == 0x8000000b, EPSR, and r2 unchanged.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\fable_p4.md:62:M_TAILRETURN's 2/23 lives. **Also found, LOW, documented-only:** the re-route's `fatal("[ SFRZ already set in PSR => ERROR ]")` is a
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\fable_p2.md:6:The seven product-code hunks are right and should ship. The repairs are all records/detector (L4 + L1) except one FILING: the diff's tail comment and the `dslot` row certify the fork's delay-slot shadow arm as "the architectural contract", and the manual says it is the wrong pipeline stage — a pre-existing upstream defect this diff makes reachable for the first time by ordinary compiled code, and which table 1 scored backwards (the mutant it "killed" produced the manual's value). Details in finding 1.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\fable_p2.md:16:- The detector: `dslot` uses `br.n +8`, so target = slot+4 = 0x1000c and the fork's SNIP (slot+4) COINCIDES with the manual's (target) — right by construction of the displacement. SFIP: fork 0x1000e (target), manual 0x10012 (target+4). Table 1's M_DSLOT (`mut_m88kexc.py`: else-arm `SFIP = SNIP + 4`) produced `sfip=0x00010012` (`killtable/M_DSLOT.out`) — the manual's value for this row — and was scored KILLED 8/9. **The row reds the architectural value and greens the fork's.** Unintended, but it is the oracle-correction rule's laundering shape and the PRETABLE's "M_DSLOT (else-arm SFIP wrong)" is a wrong record.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\fable_p2.md:17:- Disposition. Do NOT hold the fix on it: delivering with a wrong SFIP in one sub-case beats stopping every CPU, and the else-arm is a different semantic class (shadow-pointer values, not delivery) in code the diff does not touch — its own round by the reopening rule. In THIS round (L4): (a) the tail comment must not say "the common path above is already the architectural contract ... SNIP = the next one" unqualified — add "outside a delay slot; the delay-slot arm's SNIP/SFIP are upstream's guess, row m88kdslotshadow"; (b) the probe docstring/contract must label the `dslot` SFIP expectation as the fork's CURRENT else-arm value, disputed against `:2097-2104`/`:8898-8902`, held for re-pin; (c) the CHANGELOG/PRETABLE wording for M_DSLOT -> "changes the else-arm's SFIP to SNIP+4; the row reds on it — a change detector, not a correctness oracle: SNIP+4 (0x10012) is the value the manual predicts for this row". Do not re-pin SFIP to 0x10012 now (that reds the round's own fix for a reason outside the fix). FILE `m88kdslotshadow` (MEDIUM): fix = the DATA_ACCESS shape in the else-arm; rows: `dslot16` (`br.n +16`: fork today SNIP 0x1000e/SFIP 0x10016, manual 0x10016/0x1001a, no-special-case 0x1000e/0x10012 — three distinct pairs, so one row discriminates all three) and `dslotnt` (not-taken `bcnd.n` with a faulting slot: fork SFIP == SNIP, manual SFIP = SNIP+4). Re-pin FIRST (red on HEAD + this fix), then the code.
