   1: # Regression harness
   2: 
   3: ## Start here
   4: 
   5: **What this is:** a set of automated checks you run before pushing, to make sure a bug fix
   6: didn't break something that used to work. Run them all with:
   7: 
   8: ```
   9: ./run.sh
  10: ```
  11: 
  12: It prints one line per check and a final `REGRESS_PASS` or `REGRESS_FAIL`. Nothing else is
  13: required �?" no arguments, no configuration.
  14: 
  15: **What this project is:** GXemul is an emulator for old computers (1990s DECstations, SGI
  16: workstations, Sun-era hardware and so on). This is a hardened fork of version
  17: 0.7.0, carrying about 290 numbered bug fixes. Each fix has a number like `#287`, and those
  18: numbers appear throughout the code as comments and in `CHANGELOG.md`.
  19: 
  20: **Vocabulary used here**, because some of it is specific to this project:
  21: 
  22: | Term | Means |
  23: |---|---|
  24: | **gate** | one automated check, e.g. `gate_build.sh`. Passes or fails. |
  25: | **rig** | a set-up that boots a real operating system inside the emulator, so we can see whether it still works |
  26: | **pmax / arc** | the two main test machines �?" a DECstation 5000 and an Acer PICA, both running OpenBSD 2.2 from 1997 |
  27: | **pristine** | unmodified upstream GXemul 0.7.0, used as the "before" picture |
  28: | **pre-batch** | this fork as it stood before the most recent round of fixes |
  29: | **HEAD** | the current code |
  30: | **#287** etc. | a numbered bug fix; look it up in `CHANGELOG.md` |
  31: | **a check that can't fail** | a test that reports success no matter what �?" worse than no test, because it looks like coverage. Several are described below as warnings. |
  32: | **side-by-side comparison** | run the same thing on two builds and compare the results (elsewhere called a *differential*) |
  33: | **nothing extra changed** | no difference appeared outside the ones we expected |
  34: | **nothing was missed** | every difference we expected did in fact appear |
  35: | **proof the check ran** | a check that the test itself did something, so an empty or skipped run can't look like success |
  36: 
  37: If you only read one thing: **a check that cannot fail is worse than no check**, because it
  38: reports green. Most of the notes below exist because a check in this harness turned out to
  39: be one, and the details are kept so the same mistake isn't repeated.
  40: 
  41: ---
  42: 
  43: This fork carries ~290 numbered corrections against GXemul 0.7.0. The harness exists to
  44: answer one question before each push: *did a correction break something that used to
  45: work?*
  46: 
  47: ## The rule this harness is built around
  48: 
  49: > Only change what we can test for.
  50: 
  51: A gate that cannot fail is worse than no gate, because it reports green. Two gates in this
  52: tree were retired for exactly that reason and are documented here so they are not
  53: reintroduced:
  54: 
  55: * **The 20-machine `-V` smoke.** It booted twenty machines on a zero-filled blob and
  56:   quit. Round 51 instrumented it and measured that it executed **zero** floating-point
  57:   stores, so it would have passed identically whether #287 was right, wrong or absent.
  58: * **The 97-alias startup matrix.** With no kernel, every machine prints
  59:   `No filename given. Aborting.` (`core/main.c:583`), so the matrix compared *error
  60:   strings* across builds. It found exactly one real signal (`g4plus`, a fork-added CPU
  61:   subtype) and would not have caught a wrong answer anywhere.
  62: 
  63: What replaced them is below. Each gate states what it proves and, just as importantly,
  64: what it does not.
  65: 
  66: ## Gates
  67: 
  68: | # | Gate | Proves | Runtime |
  69: |---|------|--------|---------|
  70: | 1 | `gate_build.sh` | Both trees rebuild clean from committed source: exactly 223 objects (pmax) / 224 (arc), zero warnings, zero errors, and the source sync into each compile tree verified. | ~4 min |
  71: | 2 | `gate_offline.sh` | Differentials the **real, linked** `src/core/float_emul.c` against upstream. Asserts a **closed form** for the change-set plus absolute answers. This is the only gate that covers #287. | ~25 s |
  72: | 3 | `selftest_mutation.sh` | **Proves gate 2 can fail.** Reverts #287 in a scratch copy and requires the differential to go red. | ~50 s |
  73: | 4 | `gate_mips.sh` | The two primary rigs boot OpenBSD 2.2 to `uid=0` �?" pmax 15/15, arc 13/13. | ~6 min |
  74: | 5 | `gate_crossfamily.sh` | Non-MIPS CPU cores execute guest code and return checked answers: m88k to a root shell with a verified FP result, SuperH through a full kernel boot to a verified device-probe result. | ~12 min |
  75: | 6 | `gate_hygiene.sh` | No error markers in the boot logs, plus a check that the logs are real, so empty ones can't pass. | ~5 s |
  76: | 7 | `gate_ab.sh` | Three-way A/B against pristine 0.7.0 and the pre-batch fork. | ~15 min |
  77: | 8 | `gate_upstream.sh` | **Upstream's own test suite**, run three-way. The only gate this project did not author. | ~30 s |
  78: | 9 | `gate_asan_sweep.sh` | Every machine type built under AddressSanitizer and compared against upstream. The **breadth** gate. | ~6 min |
  79: 
  80: Gate 3 is the one that keeps the rest honest. Every other gate asserts something about the
  81: emulator; that one asserts something about **the harness** �?" that its strongest check
  82: responds to a broken subject. The first version of gate 2 did not, and no amount of green
  83: output would have revealed it.
  84: 
  85: `./run.sh` runs all sixteen and prints one verdict line per gate. `./run.sh 2 4` runs a subset;
  86: out-of-range selectors are rejected rather than silently running nothing.
  87: 
  88: Verdicts are `PASS`, `FAIL` and `SKIP`, and **a `SKIP` is never counted as a `PASS`** �?" it
  89: means coverage did not run, which is a gap, not evidence. A gate whose rigs partly ran is
  90: downgraded to `SKIP` rather than passing on the remainder. The skip exit code is **77**,
  91: not 2, because bash exits 2 on a syntax error: with 2 as the skip code, a typo in a gate
  92: script was silently reported as a coverage gap and the run still exited 0.
  93: 
  94: ## Why gate 2 is the strongest one here �?" and how it was nearly worthless
  95: 
  96: `ieee_store_float_value()` is pure. That means it can be differentialled offline over tens
  97: of millions of inputs in seconds, old implementation against new, which is a far stronger
  98: instrument than any boot test.
  99: 
 100: **But only because it links the real function.** The first version of this gate
 101: transcribed *both* sides of the differential into its own C file and compared the copy
 102: against itself. It never compiled, linked or executed `src/core/float_emul.c` at all �?" so
 103: deleting #287 from the shipped source would have left all eight checks green. That is the
 104: same defect as the 20-machine smoke this harness retired, one level of indirection further
 105: back, and it was caught in review rather than by the gate. Two things now prevent a repeat:
 106: the gate compiles and links the shipped `float_emul.c`, and it asserts that the file it
 107: compiled is **byte-identical to the committed one**, so "the test passed" and "the
 108: repository is correct" are the same statement.
 109: 
 110: The gate also checks **absolute answers**, not only agreement: a purely relative
 111: differential passes when both sides are wrong in the same way. `1e300` must store as
 112: `0x7f800000` (+Inf) and `-1e-40` as `0x80000000` (�^'0) �?" the values #287 exists to produce.
 113: 
 114: Beyond that, it does not merely check "did anything change" �?" it asserts that the set of
 115: inputs where old and new disagree is *exactly*:
 116: 
 117: ```
 118: old(x) != new(x)  =>  (finite && |x| >= 2^128)          // S-format overflow
 119:                   ||  (0 < |x| < 2^-126 && signbit(x))  // S-format negative underflow
 120: ```
# Regression battery

The harness used to boot�?` and shell�?`test the hardened emulator across CPU
architectures. Results live alongside it: [`BATTERY_RESULTS.md`](BATTERY_RESULTS.md)
(post�?`boot results), [`BATTERY_PASS_MAP.md`](BATTERY_PASS_MAP.md) (per�?`OS pass
map), and [`TEST_BATTERY.md`](TEST_BATTERY.md) (battery definition).

## Scripts
| script | what it does |
|---|---|
| `reg_sweep.sh` | multi�?`arch boot + per�?`machine device�?`attach assertions + panic/fatal/ASan detection |
| `reg_pmax_postboot.sh` | OpenBSD/pmax deep: root login, NAT ping, disk write/read cksum, daemon health |
| `reg_pmax_persist.sh` | reboot persistence + FFS�?`clean disk�?`integrity |
| `reg_macppc.sh` | macppc `g4` / `g4plus` boot check |
| `shell_luna88k.sh` A� `shell_landisk.sh` A� `shell_macppc.sh` | M88K / SH4 / PPC interactive�?`shell exercises |
| `drive.py` | the expect�?`style console driver the scripts use |

## Running them �?" bring your own bits
These are **illustrative research/QA scripts, not a turnkey suite.** They depend
on things deliberately *not* redistributed here:

- **A built `gxemul`** �?" `./configure && make` at the repo root (binary �+' `../gxemul`).
- **Guest kernels / images** in `KERNELS` (default `./kernels`) �?" download them
  yourself; sources are listed in [`TEST_BATTERY.md`](TEST_BATTERY.md).
- **An installed OpenBSD/pmax rig** in `RIGDIR` (default `./rig`: a `bsd` kernel +
  `disk.img`) for the pmax depth/persistence tests.

Every path is overridable via environment variables (or edit the defaults at the
top of each script):

```sh
GXEMUL=../gxemul  DRIVE=./drive.py  KERNELS=./kernels  RIGDIR=./rig  WORK=./out \
  ./reg_sweep.sh
```

> Origin is a Windows + WSL setup; treat these as a starting point to adapt to
> your environment, not a portable test framework.
